    {
      "id": "a69-i_0cXnEFt71x3FaBW",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -80.68997978326706,
      "y": 54835.938360206485,
      "width": 1639.7082263005816,
      "height": 4655.3758895098335,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bNK",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 618496834,
      "version": 893,
      "versionNonce": 483613998,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788723872928,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "* Functional Requirements\n\n- Submit an immediate or future scheduled job\n- Execute eligible jobs using horizontally scalable workers\n- Track status: SCHEDULED → READY → RUNNING \n                → terminal (COMPLETE / FAILED / CANCELLED)\n(terminal is not a status. It means the task has reached a \nfinal state and will not transition further in the normal flow.)\n\n- Retry failed or abandoned jobs with configurable policy\n- Cancel scheduled/running jobs where possible\n- Support recurring schedules\n\nFOLLOW-UP — DAG dependencies, priority scheduling\n\n\n* Non-Functional Requirements\n\nDurability — Accepted jobs must not disappear\nAvailability — Scheduling continues despite individual scheduler/worker failures\nExecution semantics — At-least-once; duplicate-safe\nSchedule latency — Eligible jobs begin within seconds under normal load\nScalability — Millions of jobs/day, tens of thousands concurrently running\n\nMUST SAY\nDurable acceptance before acknowledging submission.\nScheduler/worker failures must not permanently lose jobs.\nAt-least-once execution means duplicate handling is part of correctness.\n\n\n* Back-of-the-Envelope Estimation\nAssume 10M task executions/day\n\nAverage start rate: 10M / 86,400 ≈ 116 tasks/sec\n\n10x peak: ≈ 1,200 task starts/sec\n\nAverage execution = 60 sec\nPeak concurrent executions: 1,200 × 60 ≈ 72,000\n\n~2 KB metadata/task:\n10M × 2 KB ≈ 20 GB/day of task metadata\n\nArchitecture Implications\n\n~72K concurrent executions → workers must scale horizontally.\n~1.2K peak starts/sec → one scheduler process should not own \n                               the entire schedule space.\nMillions of future tasks → don't hold timers in memory; \n            scheduled state must remain durable/indexed.\n~20 GB/day metadata → retention/archival matters, \n                      but isn't on the hot execution path.\n\nWhat I Say\n“The important number isn't submission QPS; it's concurrency. \nAround seventy thousand simultaneous executions means workers \nare naturally horizontally scaled. And because millions of timers may \nbe pending, I want scheduling represented durably in storage rather \nthan millions of in-memory timers.”\n\n\nAPI Design\nPOST /jobs — Submit immediate, scheduled, or recurring job\n  - Req (one time scheduling): \n   { \"idempotencyKey\": \"req-12345\", \"tenantId\": \"tenant-42\", \n    \"taskType\": \"GENERATE_REPORT\", \n     \"scheduledAt\": \"2026-09-05T18:00:00Z\", \n     \"retryPolicy\": { \"maxAttempts\": 3 } \n   }\n  - Req (recurring scheduling): \n    { \"idempotencyKey\": \"req-12345\", \"tenantId\": \"tenant-42\", \n    \"taskType\": \"DAILY_REPORT\", \n     \"schedule\": { \"cron\": \"0 9 * * *\", \n    \"timezone\": \"America/Los_Angeles\" } }\n    \nGET /jobs/{jobId} — Get definition/status\n(jobId, taskType, status, scheduledAt, taskId)\nGET /tasks/{taskId} — Inspect a particular execution\nPOST /jobs/{jobId}/cancel — Cancel future/current execution\nPOST /tasks/{taskId}/retry — Explicitly retry failed execution\n\n\nData Model\nJOB(job_id PK, tenant_id, schedule/cron, payload_ref, \n      next_run_at, status, scheduler_partition, version)\n\nTASK_RUN(task_id PK, job_id FK → JOB.job_id, scheduled_for, \n             status, attempt, lease_owner, lease_until, fence_token)\n\nOUTBOX(event_id PK, task_id FK → TASK_RUN.task_id, event_type, published_at)\n\nCritical index: (scheduler_partition, status, scheduled_for) on TASK_RUN\nCritical recurring-job constraint: UNIQUE(job_id, scheduled_for) on TASK_RUN\nSubmission idempotency: UNIQUE(tenant_id, idempotency_key) on JOB",
      "fontSize": 39.6202203362539,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "* Functional Requirements\n\n- Submit an immediate or future scheduled job\n- Execute eligible jobs using horizontally scalable workers\n- Track status: SCHEDULED → READY → RUNNING \n                → terminal (COMPLETE / FAILED / CANCELLED)\n(terminal is not a status. It means the task has reached a \nfinal state and will not transition further in the normal flow.)\n\n- Retry failed or abandoned jobs with configurable policy\n- Cancel scheduled/running jobs where possible\n- Support recurring schedules\n\nFOLLOW-UP — DAG dependencies, priority scheduling\n\n\n* Non-Functional Requirements\n\nDurability — Accepted jobs must not disappear\nAvailability — Scheduling continues despite individual scheduler/worker failures\nExecution semantics — At-least-once; duplicate-safe\nSchedule latency — Eligible jobs begin within seconds under normal load\nScalability — Millions of jobs/day, tens of thousands concurrently running\n\nMUST SAY\nDurable acceptance before acknowledging submission.\nScheduler/worker failures must not permanently lose jobs.\nAt-least-once execution means duplicate handling is part of correctness.\n\n\n* Back-of-the-Envelope Estimation\nAssume 10M task executions/day\n\nAverage start rate: 10M / 86,400 ≈ 116 tasks/sec\n\n10x peak: ≈ 1,200 task starts/sec\n\nAverage execution = 60 sec\nPeak concurrent executions: 1,200 × 60 ≈ 72,000\n\n~2 KB metadata/task:\n10M × 2 KB ≈ 20 GB/day of task metadata\n\nArchitecture Implications\n\n~72K concurrent executions → workers must scale horizontally.\n~1.2K peak starts/sec → one scheduler process should not own \n                               the entire schedule space.\nMillions of future tasks → don't hold timers in memory; \n            scheduled state must remain durable/indexed.\n~20 GB/day metadata → retention/archival matters, \n                      but isn't on the hot execution path.\n\nWhat I Say\n“The important number isn't submission QPS; it's concurrency. \nAround seventy thousand simultaneous executions means workers \nare naturally horizontally scaled. And because millions of timers may \nbe pending, I want scheduling represented durably in storage rather \nthan millions of in-memory timers.”\n\n\nAPI Design\nPOST /jobs — Submit immediate, scheduled, or recurring job\n  - Req (one time scheduling): \n   { \"idempotencyKey\": \"req-12345\", \"tenantId\": \"tenant-42\", \n    \"taskType\": \"GENERATE_REPORT\", \n     \"scheduledAt\": \"2026-09-05T18:00:00Z\", \n     \"retryPolicy\": { \"maxAttempts\": 3 } \n   }\n  - Req (recurring scheduling): \n    { \"idempotencyKey\": \"req-12345\", \"tenantId\": \"tenant-42\", \n    \"taskType\": \"DAILY_REPORT\", \n     \"schedule\": { \"cron\": \"0 9 * * *\", \n    \"timezone\": \"America/Los_Angeles\" } }\n    \nGET /jobs/{jobId} — Get definition/status\n(jobId, taskType, status, scheduledAt, taskId)\nGET /tasks/{taskId} — Inspect a particular execution\nPOST /jobs/{jobId}/cancel — Cancel future/current execution\nPOST /tasks/{taskId}/retry — Explicitly retry failed execution\n\n\nData Model\nJOB(job_id PK, tenant_id, schedule/cron, payload_ref, \n      next_run_at, status, scheduler_partition, version)\n\nTASK_RUN(task_id PK, job_id FK → JOB.job_id, scheduled_for, \n             status, attempt, lease_owner, lease_until, fence_token)\n\nOUTBOX(event_id PK, task_id FK → TASK_RUN.task_id, event_type, published_at)\n\nCritical index: (scheduler_partition, status, scheduled_for) on TASK_RUN\nCritical recurring-job constraint: UNIQUE(job_id, scheduled_for) on TASK_RUN\nSubmission idempotency: UNIQUE(tenant_id, idempotency_key) on JOB",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "uuE4b8jSunDibZLpdxKeb",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 4137.922984909805,
      "y": 55071.02891978609,
      "width": 861.8433571573127,
      "height": 765.0929610689418,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bNY",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1326038878,
      "version": 706,
      "versionNonce": 957056878,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788723872928,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "HAPPY-PATH WALKTHROUGH\n\nStarting at 1, the client submits the job, and \nat 2 I persist it before acknowledging. \nAt 3 the scheduler owning that partition discovers \nthat the run is due. \nAt 4 it atomically marks it READY and creates an \noutbox record, so a crash can't lose the dispatch.”\n\n“At 5 the outbox publisher puts the task onto the \ndurable queue. \nThe worker receives it at 6, but it doesn't yet own execution. \n\nAt 7 it conditionally claims the task with a lease and \nfencing token, then executes at 8.”\n\n“At 9 it commits completion only if its fencing token is \nstill current. \n\nFinally at 10 it acknowledges the queue message. \nIf the worker disappears, the lease eventually expires \nand the task re-enters the scheduling path.",
      "fontSize": 27.82156222068881,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "HAPPY-PATH WALKTHROUGH\n\nStarting at 1, the client submits the job, and \nat 2 I persist it before acknowledging. \nAt 3 the scheduler owning that partition discovers \nthat the run is due. \nAt 4 it atomically marks it READY and creates an \noutbox record, so a crash can't lose the dispatch.”\n\n“At 5 the outbox publisher puts the task onto the \ndurable queue. \nThe worker receives it at 6, but it doesn't yet own execution. \n\nAt 7 it conditionally claims the task with a lease and \nfencing token, then executes at 8.”\n\n“At 9 it commits completion only if its fencing token is \nstill current. \n\nFinally at 10 it acknowledges the queue message. \nIf the worker disappears, the lease eventually expires \nand the task re-enters the scheduling path.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "4qyYkfArynGPl6F6xNb8a",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 3161.9875472206104,
      "y": 57363.602156943976,
      "width": 1517.2245230137055,
      "height": 2113.0375740298496,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bNZ",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1291257694,
      "version": 647,
      "versionNonce": 1766091182,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788723872928,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Why we need FENCING: A lease by itself isn't enough because \nan expired worker can wake up. So every ownership change \nincrements a fencing token. Once worker B owns token 12, \nworker A with token 11 can no longer commit authoritative completion.\n\nWhat is FENCING?\nLease alone has an important race.\n\nImagine:\n10:00 Worker A starts Task, fence = 10\n10:02 Worker A freezes maybe long GC pause/network partition\n10:07 A's lease expires\n10:08 Worker B takes Task fence = 11\n10:09 Worker B executes\n10:10 Worker A wakes up!\n\nNow both A and B may think they're executing Task 123.\nLease expiration tells us that B may take ownership.\nBut it cannot physically stop A from waking up.\nThat's why we add a fencing token.\n\nEvery new owner gets a larger token:\nWorker A -> token 10\nWorker B -> token 11\n\nDB knows: current token = 11\n\nWorker A tries: COMPLETE task\ntoken = 10\n\nDB says: REJECT, 10 != 11\n\nWorker B: COMPLETE task,  token = 11\nDB says: ACCEPT\n\nThat's fencing.\n\n",
      "fontSize": 44.48500155852316,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Why we need FENCING: A lease by itself isn't enough because \nan expired worker can wake up. So every ownership change \nincrements a fencing token. Once worker B owns token 12, \nworker A with token 11 can no longer commit authoritative completion.\n\nWhat is FENCING?\nLease alone has an important race.\n\nImagine:\n10:00 Worker A starts Task, fence = 10\n10:02 Worker A freezes maybe long GC pause/network partition\n10:07 A's lease expires\n10:08 Worker B takes Task fence = 11\n10:09 Worker B executes\n10:10 Worker A wakes up!\n\nNow both A and B may think they're executing Task 123.\nLease expiration tells us that B may take ownership.\nBut it cannot physically stop A from waking up.\nThat's why we add a fencing token.\n\nEvery new owner gets a larger token:\nWorker A -> token 10\nWorker B -> token 11\n\nDB knows: current token = 11\n\nWorker A tries: COMPLETE task\ntoken = 10\n\nDB says: REJECT, 10 != 11\n\nWorker B: COMPLETE task,  token = 11\nDB says: ACCEPT\n\nThat's fencing.\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "jdlIDv2ySpHPmfpv7ZpsA",
