    {
      "id": "2VKwGv3b1KYsuCgGjMvft",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1647.5239509540543,
      "y": 57589.0089985313,
      "width": 1427.9980210486037,
      "height": 729.14631703418,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bNg",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 991301058,
      "version": 588,
      "versionNonce": 1638309102,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788723872928,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "FINAL 45-SECOND SUMMARY\n\nThe flow is essentially 1 through 10 on the board. A client submits a job\nthrough the API, and the job database is the authoritative source of schedule\nand execution state. Scheduler shards own partitions and determine when work\nbecomes runnable; they atomically mark it READY and create an outbox event,\nwhich safely crosses into the asynchronous durable queue.”\n\n“Workers consume from that queue, but they only become the authoritative\nexecutor after a conditional DB claim that gives them a lease and fencing\ntoken. \nThey execute the task, persist completion using that fence, and then\nacknowledge the queue message. That gives us at-least-once execution without\nlosing jobs, scales through scheduler partitioning and independent worker\nscaling, and recovers crashed workers by expiring leases and feeding the task\nback through the same scheduling path.",
      "fontSize": 36.457315851709005,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "FINAL 45-SECOND SUMMARY\n\nThe flow is essentially 1 through 10 on the board. A client submits a job through the API, and the job database is the authoritative source of schedule and execution state. Scheduler shards own partitions and determine when work becomes runnable; they atomically mark it READY and create an outbox event, which safely crosses into the asynchronous durable queue.”\n\n“Workers consume from that queue, but they only become the authoritative executor after a conditional DB claim that gives them a lease and fencing token. \nThey execute the task, persist completion using that fence, and then acknowledge the queue message. That gives us at-least-once execution without losing jobs, scales through scheduler partitioning and independent worker scaling, and recovers crashed workers by expiring leases and feeding the task back through the same scheduling path.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "lPt3gar11aZt4e3iusv7C",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1686.305299711682,
      "y": 58454.785699947,
      "width": 985.0729118313111,
      "height": 354.0659304857574,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#2f9e44",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bNm",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 929571394,
      "version": 472,
      "versionNonce": 1222692654,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788723872928,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "SCHEDULED = wait until scheduled time\n\nREADY = time has arrived; waiting for worker\n\nRUNNING = some worker currently owns execution\n\nCOMPLETE = execution succeeded",
      "fontSize": 40.46467776980085,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "SCHEDULED = wait until scheduled time\n\nREADY = time has arrived; waiting for worker\n\nRUNNING = some worker currently owns execution\n\nCOMPLETE = execution succeeded",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "-al4uuJhrfUJsw02e4Lgy",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 3177.7238466410263,
      "y": 57119.707745736094,
      "width": 1276.0662992467908,
      "height": 203.10039549302746,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#f08c00",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bNn",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2079783554,
      "version": 628,
      "versionNonce": 647674222,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788723872928,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The Easiest Phrase to Remember: \n   LEASE says how long you own it. \n   FENCING proves you're still the latest owner.",
      "fontSize": 54.16010546480734,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "The Easiest Phrase to Remember: \n   LEASE says how long you own it. \n   FENCING proves you're still the latest owner.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "dsyfmgTFGcFeu-h-eAVX4",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 5066.800682099489,
      "y": 55071.506805522666,
      "width": 1262.809892259442,
      "height": 2877.255602817892,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#2f9e44",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bNo",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1943918238,
      "version": 837,
      "versionNonce": 35478446,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788723872928,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Why DB Table with partition details over Zookeeper/etcd\n\nBecause the DB is already required for job/task state, \nand it already gives us the primitives we need:\n\nconditional updates / CAS\ntransactions\nunique constraints\nlease timestamps\nversion / epoch\n\nSo instead of:\nScheduler\n   |\n   +--> Postgres\n   |\n   +--> etcd / ZooKeeper\n\nwe can initially have:\nScheduler -> Postgres   \n\nThat means fewer things to operate and fewer failure boundaries.\n\nMain benefits of using the DB: \n- no extra distributed system\n- simpler operations, easier debugging\n- task state and scheduler ownership live near each other\n- easy CAS/lease implementation\n\nsufficient when partition count and lease churn are moderate\n\nWhen would ZooKeeper/etcd be better?\nIf coordination itself becomes a high-scale or low-latency concern.\n\nFor example:\n100K+ scheduler partitions\nvery frequent ownership changes\nmany scheduler nodes\nsub-second failover requirement\nlots of ephemeral membership/leader-election operations\n\nThen etcd/ZooKeeper starts making more sense \nbecause they are built specifically for:\n- ephemeral leases/sessions\n- leader election\n- watches\n- strongly consistent coordination\n- fast ownership changes\n\nThe DB approach is simpler, but coordination \nwrites compete with normal workload.\n\nSo the trade-off is:\nDB table = simpler architecture\n\netcd / ZooKeeper =\nbetter dedicated coordination semantics\nextra infrastructure + another failure domain\n",
      "fontSize": 39.01363529244596,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Why DB Table with partition details over Zookeeper/etcd\n\nBecause the DB is already required for job/task state, \nand it already gives us the primitives we need:\n\nconditional updates / CAS\ntransactions\nunique constraints\nlease timestamps\nversion / epoch\n\nSo instead of:\nScheduler\n   |\n   +--> Postgres\n   |\n   +--> etcd / ZooKeeper\n\nwe can initially have:\nScheduler -> Postgres   \n\nThat means fewer things to operate and fewer failure boundaries.\n\nMain benefits of using the DB: \n- no extra distributed system\n- simpler operations, easier debugging\n- task state and scheduler ownership live near each other\n- easy CAS/lease implementation\n\nsufficient when partition count and lease churn are moderate\n\nWhen would ZooKeeper/etcd be better?\nIf coordination itself becomes a high-scale or low-latency concern.\n\nFor example:\n100K+ scheduler partitions\nvery frequent ownership changes\nmany scheduler nodes\nsub-second failover requirement\nlots of ephemeral membership/leader-election operations\n\nThen etcd/ZooKeeper starts making more sense \nbecause they are built specifically for:\n- ephemeral leases/sessions\n- leader election\n- watches\n- strongly consistent coordination\n- fast ownership changes\n\nThe DB approach is simpler, but coordination \nwrites compete with normal workload.\n\nSo the trade-off is:\nDB table = simpler architecture\n\netcd / ZooKeeper =\nbetter dedicated coordination semantics\nextra infrastructure + another failure domain\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
