      "locked": false,
      "text": "The main thing to tell during interviewer is:\n\n“Temporal coordinates the work, FFmpeg actually \nperforms the media processing, Kubernetes or ECS \nruns the worker fleet, and S3 stores the intermediate \nand final artifacts. We pass S3 URLs between workflow \nstages rather than moving multi-gigabyte files through \nTemporal or through our queues.”",
      "fontSize": 16.374219033190464,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "The main thing to tell during interviewer is:\n\n“Temporal coordinates the work, FFmpeg actually \nperforms the media processing, Kubernetes or ECS \nruns the worker fleet, and S3 stores the intermediate \nand final artifacts. We pass S3 URLs between workflow \nstages rather than moving multi-gigabyte files through \nTemporal or through our queues.”",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "1my_HJ0VM3DcKOj-HJsO4",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 3651.3892422539316,
      "y": 83884.03777333486,
      "width": 1147.7763477268322,
      "height": 347.08750464298384,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bXY",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 112214135,
      "version": 433,
      "versionNonce": 516351159,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790043824225,
      "created": 1790041519883,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "| DAG step                   | Real implementation                                        | Typical technology                               |\n| ----------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------- |\n| Detect completed upload | Event emitted after original object arrives           | S3 Event Notification, EventBridge or SQS |\n| Start processing           | Create one durable workflow per uploaded video     | **Temporal**                                      |\n| Inspect video               | Determine codec, duration, dimensions, streams      | **FFprobe**                                       |\n| Split/process source       | Create GOP/segment boundaries or prepare chunks | **FFmpeg**                                       |\n| Transcode video            | Encode different resolutions/bitrates/codecs        | **FFmpeg** running on CPU/GPU workers  |\n| Audio processing            | Extract/normalize/transcode audio                    | FFmpeg                                            |\n| Thumbnail generation       | Extract selected frames                                 | FFmpeg                                            |\n| Speech-to-text              | Generate captions/transcript                           | AWS Transcribe or ML service                 |\n| Package ABR output       | Build HLS/DASH/CMAF segments and manifests     | FFmpeg, Shaka Packager, or MediaConvert  |\n| Store artifacts              | Original, intermediate and final outputs              | S3                                                   |\n| Update status               | `PROCESSING → READY`                             | Metadata DB                                     |\n| Deliver video                 | Cache/serve segments globally                          | CloudFront/CDN                                  |\n",
      "fontSize": 18.51133358095914,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "| DAG step                   | Real implementation                                        | Typical technology                               |\n| ----------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------- |\n| Detect completed upload | Event emitted after original object arrives           | S3 Event Notification, EventBridge or SQS |\n| Start processing           | Create one durable workflow per uploaded video     | **Temporal**                                      |\n| Inspect video               | Determine codec, duration, dimensions, streams      | **FFprobe**                                       |\n| Split/process source       | Create GOP/segment boundaries or prepare chunks | **FFmpeg**                                       |\n| Transcode video            | Encode different resolutions/bitrates/codecs        | **FFmpeg** running on CPU/GPU workers  |\n| Audio processing            | Extract/normalize/transcode audio                    | FFmpeg                                            |\n| Thumbnail generation       | Extract selected frames                                 | FFmpeg                                            |\n| Speech-to-text              | Generate captions/transcript                           | AWS Transcribe or ML service                 |\n| Package ABR output       | Build HLS/DASH/CMAF segments and manifests     | FFmpeg, Shaka Packager, or MediaConvert  |\n| Store artifacts              | Original, intermediate and final outputs              | S3                                                   |\n| Update status               | `PROCESSING → READY`                             | Metadata DB                                     |\n| Deliver video                 | Cache/serve segments globally                          | CloudFront/CDN                                  |\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "SN3WXwltG61X6PJnZGC3g",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 3655.0807254168512,
      "y": 83826.1282205784,
      "width": 342.86308930945546,
      "height": 26.18716190867787,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bXb",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 161435289,
      "version": 66,
      "versionNonce": 873936343,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790043824225,
      "created": 1790041873203,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "What does each box actually run?",
      "fontSize": 20.949729526942296,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "What does each box actually run?",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "ujHOLcRzGpSlZRyqV4QGm",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1761.1693193604096,
      "y": 83662.54085387225,
      "width": 711.3795166015625,
      "height": 725,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bXc",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1193089241,
      "version": 247,
      "versionNonce": 547562777,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790043829430,
      "created": 1790043617748,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Interview script:\n\n“Once the original upload completes in S3, an object-created \nevent starts a durable video-processing workflow. I would use \nsomething like Temporal as the DAG orchestrator.\n\nTemporal itself doesn't process the media. It schedules work \nto different worker pools. We first inspect and logically split \nthe video using FFprobe or FFmpeg. Then the expensive work \nfans out across many FFmpeg workers, which generate multiple \nrenditions such as 360p, 720p and 1080p. Audio, thumbnails \nand transcription can run in parallel as well.\n\nThese workers can run as containers on Kubernetes or ECS, \nand the transcoding pools can scale independently based on \ntask backlog and CPU or GPU utilization.\n\nLarge intermediate files are always written to S3. The workflow \nonly passes S3 object references, not the actual media bytes.\n\nOnce all required renditions complete, the workflow fans back \nin and a packaging step generates HLS or DASH manifests \nreferencing the different segments. We store those manifests \nand segments in S3, update the video's metadata to READY, \nand then serve the assets through the CDN.\n\nThe key reason for the DAG is that transcoding is CPU-intensive \nbut highly parallelizable. Temporal gives us dependency management, \nretries and durable execution, while FFmpeg does the actual media work.”",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Interview script:\n\n“Once the original upload completes in S3, an object-created \nevent starts a durable video-processing workflow. I would use \nsomething like Temporal as the DAG orchestrator.\n\nTemporal itself doesn't process the media. It schedules work \nto different worker pools. We first inspect and logically split \nthe video using FFprobe or FFmpeg. Then the expensive work \nfans out across many FFmpeg workers, which generate multiple \nrenditions such as 360p, 720p and 1080p. Audio, thumbnails \nand transcription can run in parallel as well.\n\nThese workers can run as containers on Kubernetes or ECS, \nand the transcoding pools can scale independently based on \ntask backlog and CPU or GPU utilization.\n\nLarge intermediate files are always written to S3. The workflow \nonly passes S3 object references, not the actual media bytes.\n\nOnce all required renditions complete, the workflow fans back \nin and a packaging step generates HLS or DASH manifests \nreferencing the different segments. We store those manifests \nand segments in S3, update the video's metadata to READY, \nand then serve the assets through the CDN.\n\nThe key reason for the DAG is that transcoding is CPU-intensive \nbut highly parallelizable. Temporal gives us dependency management, \nretries and durable execution, while FFmpeg does the actual media work.”",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1366,
      "versionNonce": 182723607,
      "index": "bZ0",
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "0WLZMEr84ym4wMJQyTGDK",
      "fillStyle": "hachure",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 3939.1967891324066,
      "y": 85145.07327023051,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 1582.5250525446947,
      "height": 1292.7274090114454,
      "seed": 631837431,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790044022651,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "fontSize": 41.36727708836625,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "text": "Workflow with Multiple Kafka Topics\n\nIngestion/Chunking Queue:\n - Incoming video streams are published to this topic by the media server.\n\nChunking Stage: \n- Consumes from the chunking queue and Divide video streams into smaller\nchunks.\n\nSegmenting Stage:\n - SegmentingWorker: Consumes from the SegmentingQueue.\n - Segmenting Output: Process chunks into precise segments.\n\nTranscoding Stage:\n - TranscodingWorker: Consumes from the Transcoding Queue.\n - Transcoding Output: Convert segments into multiple resolutions and \n    bitrates.\n\nABR Packaging Stage:\n - ABR Packaging Worker: Consumes from the ABR Packaging queue.\n - ABR Packaging Output: \n        * Package segments for adaptive bitrate streaming.\n        * Create Manifest files and store in S3.\n        * Store metadata about video segments to DDB (this could be direct\nREST call, i.e. another service that takes the metadata and store in DDb) ",
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Workflow with Multiple Kafka Topics\n\nIngestion/Chunking Queue:\n - Incoming video streams are published to this topic by the media server.\n\nChunking Stage: \n- Consumes from the chunking queue and Divide video streams into smaller chunks.\n\nSegmenting Stage:\n - SegmentingWorker: Consumes from the SegmentingQueue.\n - Segmenting Output: Process chunks into precise segments.\n\nTranscoding Stage:\n - TranscodingWorker: Consumes from the Transcoding Queue.\n - Transcoding Output: Convert segments into multiple resolutions and \n    bitrates.\n\nABR Packaging Stage:\n - ABR Packaging Worker: Consumes from the ABR Packaging queue.\n - ABR Packaging Output: \n        * Package segments for adaptive bitrate streaming.\n        * Create Manifest files and store in S3.\n        * Store metadata about video segments to DDB (this could be direct REST call, i.e. another service that takes the metadata and store in DDb) ",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": 1790043981050,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
