    {
      "id": "B1947xFGHpObxRcPB_deW",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1281.9321799183044,
      "y": 87808.83815724486,
      "width": 550.4365463654951,
      "height": 547.3715684893976,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bbS",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1664290615,
      "version": 79,
      "versionNonce": 2014161977,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790100123408,
      "created": 1790099712938,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How does the View API scale to tens of millions of concurrent reads?\n\nInterview script: \nFor tens of millions of concurrent View API requests, my main goal \nis to make sure the majority of those requests never hit the database.\n\nI would keep the Event Service stateless and horizontally scale many \ninstances behind a load balancer.\n\nIn front of the database, I’d use Redis as a distributed cache. Event \ndetails, performer information, venue metadata, and seat-map information \nare very read-heavy but change infrequently, so they are excellent cache \ncandidates.\n\nThe read path becomes: client to load balancer, then Event Service, then \nRedis. On a cache hit we return immediately. On a miss, we read from the \ndatabase, populate Redis, and return the response.\n\nI’d use longer TTLs for static data such as venue information and shorter \nTTLs for more dynamic data. When event metadata changes, the application \npublishes an invalidation event so the corresponding cache entry is removed \nor refreshed.\n\nFor additional protection, I’d use database read replicas, cache pre-warming \nfor major events, and stampede protection so millions of simultaneous cache \nmisses don’t overwhelm the database.\n\nSo the scaling strategy is essentially: stateless horizontal scaling at the \nservice layer, aggressive distributed caching, and keeping the database off \nthe hot read path.",
      "fontSize": 14.59657515971727,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How does the View API scale to tens of millions of concurrent reads?\n\nInterview script: \nFor tens of millions of concurrent View API requests, my main goal \nis to make sure the majority of those requests never hit the database.\n\nI would keep the Event Service stateless and horizontally scale many \ninstances behind a load balancer.\n\nIn front of the database, I’d use Redis as a distributed cache. Event \ndetails, performer information, venue metadata, and seat-map information \nare very read-heavy but change infrequently, so they are excellent cache \ncandidates.\n\nThe read path becomes: client to load balancer, then Event Service, then \nRedis. On a cache hit we return immediately. On a miss, we read from the \ndatabase, populate Redis, and return the response.\n\nI’d use longer TTLs for static data such as venue information and shorter \nTTLs for more dynamic data. When event metadata changes, the application \npublishes an invalidation event so the corresponding cache entry is removed \nor refreshed.\n\nFor additional protection, I’d use database read replicas, cache pre-warming \nfor major events, and stampede protection so millions of simultaneous cache \nmisses don’t overwhelm the database.\n\nSo the scaling strategy is essentially: stateless horizontal scaling at the \nservice layer, aggressive distributed caching, and keeping the database off \nthe hot read path.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "eBEmlHTnG9xe1e5dEsLEn",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1489.320164813728,
      "y": 87546.46632935405,
      "width": 134.1195526123047,
      "height": 53.63412166669147,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bbU",
