    },
    {
      "id": "agR1efX6L5m1BTcxxAIis",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1906.6193442371602,
      "y": 87809.9267168999,
      "width": 648.7625732421875,
      "height": 521.2046788610031,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#2f9e44",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bbW",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 52562649,
      "version": 151,
      "versionNonce": 576177145,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790100957152,
      "created": 1790100126884,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How will the system ensure a good user experience during \nhigh-demand events with millions simultaneously booking tickets?\n\nInterview script\n\n“For a very high-demand event, I would not allow millions of users \nto reach the seat-selection and booking services simultaneously.\n\nI’d put a virtual waiting room in front of the booking flow. Users \nenter a Redis-backed queue, for example a sorted set ordered by \narrival time.\n\nI’d maintain an SSE connection to push real-time queue position \nand estimated wait time to the client. SSE is a good fit because \nthis is primarily server-to-client communication.\n\nAn Admission Controller gradually releases users based on the capacity \nof the downstream booking system. When a user is admitted, we issue \na short-lived admission token or store their session in Redis.\n\nThe Booking Service validates that admission before allowing seat \nreservation, so users cannot bypass the queue.\n\nThis gives us controlled backpressure, protects the booking and inventory \nsystems from overload, and still gives users real-time feedback while they wait.”",
      "fontSize": 16.678549723552102,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How will the system ensure a good user experience during \nhigh-demand events with millions simultaneously booking tickets?\n\nInterview script\n\n“For a very high-demand event, I would not allow millions of users \nto reach the seat-selection and booking services simultaneously.\n\nI’d put a virtual waiting room in front of the booking flow. Users \nenter a Redis-backed queue, for example a sorted set ordered by \narrival time.\n\nI’d maintain an SSE connection to push real-time queue position \nand estimated wait time to the client. SSE is a good fit because \nthis is primarily server-to-client communication.\n\nAn Admission Controller gradually releases users based on the capacity \nof the downstream booking system. When a user is admitted, we issue \na short-lived admission token or store their session in Redis.\n\nThe Booking Service validates that admission before allowing seat \nreservation, so users cannot bypass the queue.\n\nThis gives us controlled backpressure, protects the booking and inventory \nsystems from overload, and still gives users real-time feedback while they wait.”",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "pj_bIAH_iPXXE_bSJHApk",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2581.6318764186112,
      "y": 87791.80914689855,
      "width": 578.6331522939846,
      "height": 558.1831515601607,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bbX",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 150348377,
      "version": 420,
      "versionNonce": 1543289143,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790100963808,
      "created": 1790100345424,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How can you speed up frequently repeated search queries \nand reduce load on our search infrastructure?\n\nInterview Script\nI would avoid running complex search queries directly against \nour transactional PostgreSQL database.\n\nPostgreSQL remains the source of truth, but I’d introduce \nElasticsearch as a dedicated search index.\n\nElasticsearch uses inverted indexes, which makes full-text search, \nfiltering, autocomplete, and fuzzy matching significantly faster \nthan scanning relational data.\n\nFor example, if a user searches for ‘Tayler Swift,’ Elasticsearch \ncan use fuzzy matching to still return ‘Taylor Swift.’\n\nTo keep the search index synchronized, I’d use CDC—typically \nDebezium reading PostgreSQL changes, publishing them through Kafka, \nand a search indexer updating Elasticsearch.\n\nThis means search becomes eventually consistent by a few seconds, \nwhich is normally acceptable for event discovery, while the transactional \ndatabase remains authoritative.\n\nSo the key idea is: PostgreSQL for correctness, Elasticsearch for fast \nsearch, and CDC for synchronization.\n",
      "fontSize": 15.94809004457604,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How can you speed up frequently repeated search queries \nand reduce load on our search infrastructure?\n\nInterview Script\nI would avoid running complex search queries directly against \nour transactional PostgreSQL database.\n\nPostgreSQL remains the source of truth, but I’d introduce \nElasticsearch as a dedicated search index.\n\nElasticsearch uses inverted indexes, which makes full-text search, \nfiltering, autocomplete, and fuzzy matching significantly faster \nthan scanning relational data.\n\nFor example, if a user searches for ‘Tayler Swift,’ Elasticsearch \ncan use fuzzy matching to still return ‘Taylor Swift.’\n\nTo keep the search index synchronized, I’d use CDC—typically \nDebezium reading PostgreSQL changes, publishing them through Kafka, \nand a search indexer updating Elasticsearch.\n\nThis means search becomes eventually consistent by a few seconds, \nwhich is normally acceptable for event discovery, while the transactional \ndatabase remains authoritative.\n\nSo the key idea is: PostgreSQL for correctness, Elasticsearch for fast \nsearch, and CDC for synchronization.\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "9UnBAO4Wd_UbrgFLNdslE",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2435.907438740187,
      "y": 86899.16919740377,
      "width": 268.88658649650324,
      "height": 258.9699631649855,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bbY",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 500590615,
      "version": 306,
      "versionNonce": 855052249,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790100985414,
      "created": 1790100536389,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Postgres to Elastic writes flow\nClient --> Event Service --> PostgreSQL\n                         |\n                         | CDC\n                         v\n                    Kafka/Debezium\n                         |\n                         v\n                    Elasticsearch\n                         ^\n                         |\n                  Search Service\n                         ^\n                         |\n                      Client",
      "fontSize": 13.811731368799236,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Postgres to Elastic writes flow\nClient --> Event Service --> PostgreSQL\n                         |\n                         | CDC\n                         v\n                    Kafka/Debezium\n                         |\n                         v\n                    Elasticsearch\n                         ^\n                         |\n                  Search Service\n                         ^\n                         |\n                      Client",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "5Mb8mUInlZeZ8RYruFqbG",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2562.2634406229413,
      "y": 87225.69594117223,
      "width": 535.9139782716836,
      "height": 516.5368214704358,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#f08c00",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bbZ",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 763183671,
      "version": 177,
      "versionNonce": 1073890231,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790101009628,
      "created": 1790100764585,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How can you speed up frequently repeated search queries \nand reduce load on our search infrastructure?\n    strategy is: \n    - CDN for global hot queries, \n    - Redis for application-level hot queries, and \n    - Elasticsearch’s internal caches underneath.\n\n\nInterview script\nElasticsearch already provides shard-level query and request \ncaches, so repeated filters and search responses can often \nbe served without recomputing the entire query.\n\nFor very hot searches, I can also add a Redis cache in front of \nElasticsearch using a normalized query as the key and a short TTL.\n\nAt even larger scale, non-personalized searches can be cached at the \nCDN, for example CloudFront, so requests can be served geographically \nclose to the user without reaching our backend.\n\nThe main trade-off is freshness. Search results are eventually consistent \nanyway, so I’d use short TTLs rather than trying to perfectly invalidate \nevery possible query when an event changes.\n\nSo the strategy is: \n    CDN for global hot queries, \n    Redis for application-level hot queries, and \n    Elasticsearch’s internal caches underneath.",
      "fontSize": 14.758194899155297,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How can you speed up frequently repeated search queries \nand reduce load on our search infrastructure?\n    strategy is: \n    - CDN for global hot queries, \n    - Redis for application-level hot queries, and \n    - Elasticsearch’s internal caches underneath.\n\n\nInterview script\nElasticsearch already provides shard-level query and request \ncaches, so repeated filters and search responses can often \nbe served without recomputing the entire query.\n\nFor very hot searches, I can also add a Redis cache in front of \nElasticsearch using a normalized query as the key and a short TTL.\n\nAt even larger scale, non-personalized searches can be cached at the \nCDN, for example CloudFront, so requests can be served geographically \nclose to the user without reaching our backend.\n\nThe main trade-off is freshness. Search results are eventually consistent \nanyway, so I’d use short TTLs rather than trying to perfectly invalidate \nevery possible query when an event changes.\n\nSo the strategy is: \n    CDN for global hot queries, \n    Redis for application-level hot queries, and \n    Elasticsearch’s internal caches underneath.",
