    {
      "id": "zs5GBVjcljci1jPpaxX__",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -710.9719054572302,
      "y": 88949.17257615773,
      "width": 1200.2666919549838,
      "height": 750.166682471864,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#f08c00",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bcn",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1988003709,
      "version": 137,
      "versionNonce": 2140523923,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1793727934127,
      "created": 1793727907258,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Step-by-step example:\n1. Truck truck-456 sends a GPS update to the Ingestion API.\n2. The API authenticates the GPS device and determines its identity.\n3. It checks vehicle/device ownership information, potentially through\nPostgreSQL or a cached lookup:\n   - Is this device registered?\n   - Which vehicle does it belong to?\n   - Which tenant owns the vehicle?\n   - Is the device authorized to submit GPS updates?\n4. Once validated, the API publishes the GPS event to Kafka.\n5. After Kafka durably acknowledges the event, the API acknowledges the\ndevice.\nDoes this insert anything into PostgreSQL?\n\nNot during normal GPS ingestion.\n\nPostgreSQL writes happen through separate administrative operations, such as\nwhen a vehicle is registered, a GPS device is assigned to a vehicle, or its\nconfiguration changes. The attached design doesn't explicitly define those\nadministrative API endpoints.",
      "fontSize": 30.00666729887458,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Step-by-step example:\n1. Truck truck-456 sends a GPS update to the Ingestion API.\n2. The API authenticates the GPS device and determines its identity.\n3. It checks vehicle/device ownership information, potentially through PostgreSQL or a cached lookup:\n   - Is this device registered?\n   - Which vehicle does it belong to?\n   - Which tenant owns the vehicle?\n   - Is the device authorized to submit GPS updates?\n4. Once validated, the API publishes the GPS event to Kafka.\n5. After Kafka durably acknowledges the event, the API acknowledges the device.\nDoes this insert anything into PostgreSQL?\n\nNot during normal GPS ingestion.\n\nPostgreSQL writes happen through separate administrative operations, such as when a vehicle is registered, a GPS device is assigned to a vehicle, or its configuration changes. The attached design doesn't explicitly define those administrative API endpoints.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
