    {
      "id": "rPai0Iw4WprBZ4o455PHR",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -814.4541126890523,
      "y": 89778.20612357519,
      "width": 1299.9138276217116,
      "height": 1218.6692133953543,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#e03131",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bcz",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1179084051,
      "version": 80,
      "versionNonce": 1646208093,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1793729691308,
      "created": 1793729682221,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Why do we need a WebSocket Gateway?\nSuppose truck T101 sends updated GPS coordinates every 5 seconds.\n\nWithout WebSockets, the browser would need to repeatedly call the Map API to\nfind its new position.\n\nFor example:\n- 10:00:00 — Browser calls Map API.\n- 10:00:05 — Browser calls Map API again.\n- 10:00:10 — Browser calls Map API again.\n- 10:00:15 — Browser calls Map API again.\nNow imagine 10,000 dispatchers doing this repeatedly, even when nothing\nchanges.\n\nThis creates unnecessary HTTP requests and Redis reads.\n\nWebSockets solve this problem by allowing the backend to send updates to the\nbrowser automatically over an existing connection.\n\nHere's what happens instead:\n1. The browser establishes a persistent WebSocket connection to the WebSocket\nGateway.\n2. It subscribes to location changes for fleet 123 (after authorization).\n3. Truck T101 sends new GPS coordinates to the Ingestion API.\n4. The update flows through Kafka to the Location Processor.\n5. The Location Processor updates Redis and sends the latest-position change\nto the WebSocket distribution layer.\n6. The WebSocket Gateway pushes the update to subscribed browsers.\n7. The browser moves T101's marker on the map.\nThe dispatcher does not have to request the new location repeatedly.",
      "fontSize": 32.49784569054282,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Why do we need a WebSocket Gateway?\nSuppose truck T101 sends updated GPS coordinates every 5 seconds.\n\nWithout WebSockets, the browser would need to repeatedly call the Map API to find its new position.\n\nFor example:\n- 10:00:00 — Browser calls Map API.\n- 10:00:05 — Browser calls Map API again.\n- 10:00:10 — Browser calls Map API again.\n- 10:00:15 — Browser calls Map API again.\nNow imagine 10,000 dispatchers doing this repeatedly, even when nothing changes.\n\nThis creates unnecessary HTTP requests and Redis reads.\n\nWebSockets solve this problem by allowing the backend to send updates to the browser automatically over an existing connection.\n\nHere's what happens instead:\n1. The browser establishes a persistent WebSocket connection to the WebSocket Gateway.\n2. It subscribes to location changes for fleet 123 (after authorization).\n3. Truck T101 sends new GPS coordinates to the Ingestion API.\n4. The update flows through Kafka to the Location Processor.\n5. The Location Processor updates Redis and sends the latest-position change to the WebSocket distribution layer.\n6. The WebSocket Gateway pushes the update to subscribed browsers.\n7. The browser moves T101's marker on the map.\nThe dispatcher does not have to request the new location repeatedly.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
