    {
      "id": "GZUpLiy3V8-KOr2VQ7AUv",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -802.7285358621859,
      "y": 91055.88478493213,
      "width": 975.477784171791,
      "height": 4359.16634801769,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#2f9e44",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bd3",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1644803773,
      "version": 354,
      "versionNonce": 478504733,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1793731226677,
      "created": 1793730777816,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "# Fleet Tracking System — Quick Revision Notes\n\n## 1. Functional Requirements\n\n**Core**\n- Vehicles publish GPS location, speed, heading, and timestamp continuously.\n- Dispatchers view live vehicle positions on a map.\n- Retrieve latest locations for a single vehicle, entire fleet, or map viewport.\n- Retrieve historical GPS trails and replay trips.\n\n**Follow-ups**\n- Geofencing, route deviation alerts, ETA estimation.\n- Driver-vehicle assignment and dispatch optimization.\n\n## 2. Non-Functional Requirements\n\n- **Low latency:** GPS updates visible within a few seconds.\n- **High availability:** Recover delayed GPS data after network outages.\n- **Scalability:** Support high-volume GPS ingestion and concurrent viewers.\n- **Multi-tenancy:** Strict fleet-level data isolation and authorization.\n\n## 3. Back-of-the-Envelope Estimation\n\nActive vehicles              = 100,000\nGPS interval                 = 5 seconds\n\nGPS writes/sec               = 100K / 5\n                             = 20,000 events/sec\n\nGPS events/day               = 20K × 86,400\n                             = 1.728 billion events\n\nStorage/day (200 B/event)    ≈ 346 GB raw\n30-day raw history           ≈ 10.4 TB\n\nLive dispatcher connections  = 10,000 (assumed)\n\n\n*Storage excludes replication, indexing, and compression. Peak traffic may exceed\nthe average.*\n\n### Architecture Implications\n\n- **20K events/sec:** Partitioned ingestion + parallel consumers.\n- **1.7B events/day:** Separate latest-location storage from historical storage.\n- **10K dispatchers:** WebSocket connections + selective fan-out (only relevant\nvehicle updates).\n\n**Interview Script:**\n\n\"The key observation is that GPS writes are continuous, but our read patterns\ndiffer. Dispatchers need the latest position with low latency, while historical\nplayback requires efficient time-range queries. I'll optimize these two access\npatterns separately.\"\n\n\n## 4. Core APIs\nGET /fleets/{fleetId}/vehicles/locations : \n            Latest fleet positions, optional viewport filtering\nGET /vehicles/{vehicleId}/history?from=&to= : \n            Historical GPS trail\n\nvehicle.location : GPS location event\n\nWS /ws/fleets/{fleetId}/locations : Real-time location subscription\n\n\n## 5. GPS Event Schema\n\n```json\n{\n  \"tenantId\": \"fleet-123\",\n  \"vehicleId\": \"truck-456\",\n  \"timestamp\": 1793635200000,\n  \"sequence\": 1052,\n  \"latitude\": 37.7749,\n  \"longitude\": -122.4194,\n  \"speedKph\": 65,\n  \"heading\": 120\n}\n```\n\n**Important:** The ingestion service derives trusted `tenantId` and\n`vehicleId` from authenticated device identity. Never trust device-supplied\nidentifiers.\n\n## 6. Core Data Models\n\n### VEHICLE — Authoritative Metadata\n- `vehicleId` — Unique vehicle identifier.\n- `tenantId` — Fleet ownership and authorization scope.\n- `deviceId` — Associated authenticated GPS device.\n- `status` — Active, inactive, decommissioned.\n\n### LATEST_LOCATION — Fast Live Reads\n- `vehicleId`, `tenantId`\n- `latitude`, `longitude`\n- `eventTimestamp`, `sequence`\n- `speed`, `heading`\n\n**Purpose:** Store only the newest accepted location per vehicle for fast map\nqueries.\n\n**Important:** Reject duplicate/stale updates using sequence and timestamp\nordering. Rebuildable from durable GPS events.\n\n### LOCATION_HISTORY — Historical Tracking\n- `tenantId`, `vehicleId`\n- `eventTimestamp`, `sequence`\n- `latitude`, `longitude`, `speed`\n\n**Purpose:** Durable, append-oriented GPS records for trip replay, auditing, and\nrecovery.\n\n**Access pattern:** Query by vehicle and time range.\n\n### FLEET_MEMBERSHIP — Access Control\n- `tenantId`, `userId`\n- `role`, `membershipStatus`\n\n**Purpose:** Authoritative fleet-user mapping for tenant isolation and role-\nbased access.\n\n## 7. Key Interview Takeaways\n\n1. **Separate latest location from history:** Different access patterns require\ndifferent storage optimizations.\n2. **Partition GPS ingestion:** Handle high write throughput with parallel\nprocessing.\n3. **Handle out-of-order events:** Use sequence/timestamp ordering to protect\nlatest-location correctness.\n4. **Use WebSockets for live updates:** Push changes instead of repeated\npolling; filter by authorized fleet/viewport.\n5. **Persist durable GPS history:** Support replay and recovery after\ndownstream failures.\n6. **Enforce tenant isolation:** Authenticate devices and authorize all\ndispatcher queries/subscriptions.\n\n**One-line design summary:**\n\n\"Build a durable, partitioned GPS ingestion pipeline, maintain a fast latest-\nlocation read model, persist append-oriented historical events, and push\nauthorized real-time updates to dispatchers through WebSockets.\"",
      "fontSize": 24.38694460429477,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "# Fleet Tracking System — Quick Revision Notes\n\n## 1. Functional Requirements\n\n**Core**\n- Vehicles publish GPS location, speed, heading, and timestamp continuously.\n- Dispatchers view live vehicle positions on a map.\n- Retrieve latest locations for a single vehicle, entire fleet, or map viewport.\n- Retrieve historical GPS trails and replay trips.\n\n**Follow-ups**\n- Geofencing, route deviation alerts, ETA estimation.\n- Driver-vehicle assignment and dispatch optimization.\n\n## 2. Non-Functional Requirements\n\n- **Low latency:** GPS updates visible within a few seconds.\n- **High availability:** Recover delayed GPS data after network outages.\n- **Scalability:** Support high-volume GPS ingestion and concurrent viewers.\n- **Multi-tenancy:** Strict fleet-level data isolation and authorization.\n\n## 3. Back-of-the-Envelope Estimation\n\nActive vehicles              = 100,000\nGPS interval                 = 5 seconds\n\nGPS writes/sec               = 100K / 5\n                             = 20,000 events/sec\n\nGPS events/day               = 20K × 86,400\n                             = 1.728 billion events\n\nStorage/day (200 B/event)    ≈ 346 GB raw\n30-day raw history           ≈ 10.4 TB\n\nLive dispatcher connections  = 10,000 (assumed)\n\n\n*Storage excludes replication, indexing, and compression. Peak traffic may exceed the average.*\n\n### Architecture Implications\n\n- **20K events/sec:** Partitioned ingestion + parallel consumers.\n- **1.7B events/day:** Separate latest-location storage from historical storage.\n- **10K dispatchers:** WebSocket connections + selective fan-out (only relevant vehicle updates).\n\n**Interview Script:**\n\n\"The key observation is that GPS writes are continuous, but our read patterns differ. Dispatchers need the latest position with low latency, while historical playback requires efficient time-range queries. I'll optimize these two access patterns separately.\"\n\n\n## 4. Core APIs\nGET /fleets/{fleetId}/vehicles/locations : \n            Latest fleet positions, optional viewport filtering\nGET /vehicles/{vehicleId}/history?from=&to= : \n            Historical GPS trail\n\nvehicle.location : GPS location event\n\nWS /ws/fleets/{fleetId}/locations : Real-time location subscription\n\n\n## 5. GPS Event Schema\n\n```json\n{\n  \"tenantId\": \"fleet-123\",\n  \"vehicleId\": \"truck-456\",\n  \"timestamp\": 1793635200000,\n  \"sequence\": 1052,\n  \"latitude\": 37.7749,\n  \"longitude\": -122.4194,\n  \"speedKph\": 65,\n  \"heading\": 120\n}\n```\n\n**Important:** The ingestion service derives trusted `tenantId` and `vehicleId` from authenticated device identity. Never trust device-supplied identifiers.\n\n## 6. Core Data Models\n\n### VEHICLE — Authoritative Metadata\n- `vehicleId` — Unique vehicle identifier.\n- `tenantId` — Fleet ownership and authorization scope.\n- `deviceId` — Associated authenticated GPS device.\n- `status` — Active, inactive, decommissioned.\n\n### LATEST_LOCATION — Fast Live Reads\n- `vehicleId`, `tenantId`\n- `latitude`, `longitude`\n- `eventTimestamp`, `sequence`\n- `speed`, `heading`\n\n**Purpose:** Store only the newest accepted location per vehicle for fast map queries.\n\n**Important:** Reject duplicate/stale updates using sequence and timestamp ordering. Rebuildable from durable GPS events.\n\n### LOCATION_HISTORY — Historical Tracking\n- `tenantId`, `vehicleId`\n- `eventTimestamp`, `sequence`\n- `latitude`, `longitude`, `speed`\n\n**Purpose:** Durable, append-oriented GPS records for trip replay, auditing, and recovery.\n\n**Access pattern:** Query by vehicle and time range.\n\n### FLEET_MEMBERSHIP — Access Control\n- `tenantId`, `userId`\n- `role`, `membershipStatus`\n\n**Purpose:** Authoritative fleet-user mapping for tenant isolation and role-based access.\n\n## 7. Key Interview Takeaways\n\n1. **Separate latest location from history:** Different access patterns require different storage optimizations.\n2. **Partition GPS ingestion:** Handle high write throughput with parallel processing.\n3. **Handle out-of-order events:** Use sequence/timestamp ordering to protect latest-location correctness.\n4. **Use WebSockets for live updates:** Push changes instead of repeated polling; filter by authorized fleet/viewport.\n5. **Persist durable GPS history:** Support replay and recovery after downstream failures.\n6. **Enforce tenant isolation:** Authenticate devices and authorize all dispatcher queries/subscriptions.\n\n**One-line design summary:**\n\n\"Build a durable, partitioned GPS ingestion pipeline, maintain a fast latest-location read model, persist append-oriented historical events, and push authorized real-time updates to dispatchers through WebSockets.\"",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
